00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.220
LORENA CROWLEY:
Welcome to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.920
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.930
We host webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.050
My name is Lorena
Crowley, and I'm here

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:24.290
today to talk to you about
managing Chrome Browser

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.990
updates in your enterprise.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.390
As enterprises look to
adopt Chrome Browser

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.480
or make it its primary
browser, the topic

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.810
of how to manage
updates comes up often.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.960
In today's session, we'll talk
through some of your options

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.170
for managing updates
and give you some tips

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.570
so that you can best
deploy auto-updates

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.610
in your environment.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.710
Before we get
started, let's talk

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.140
a little bit about
why the browser is

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.400
growing in importance.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.140
You may have already
noticed some shifts

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.420
in your own enterprise
that impact the way

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.940
you think about the web browser.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.730
Research shows
that users leverage

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:11.650
22 cloud and SAS-based
apps across three devices

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.980
to get their jobs done.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.650
We did some recent
research with Forrester

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:20.250
that revealed the average
user spends about a third

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.060
of their day in the web browser.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.880
And there's an even
more connected group

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.780
of users that spend
upwards of four hours a day

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.270
getting work done
in their browser.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:36.030
We'll talk about that group
of users in a little bit.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.320
And we know this trend
is likely on the rise.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.590
81% of IT professionals
report that they'll

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:48.630
continue to view cloud and
SAS app adoption as a priority

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:51.120
when they explore the
adoption of new apps

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.370
in their environment.

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:59.260
I talked about this idea of a
more connected type of worker.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:03.500
At Google Cloud, we call these
members of the workforce cloud

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:04.880
workers.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.930
These cloud workers are highly
reliant on browser and web

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.600
applications to
get their jobs done

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.820
and also have a highly remote
and mobile working style.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:20.330
One in four workers are
actually already considered

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.070
cloud workers.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:25.340
And some of you might consider
yourself cloud workers.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.310
Because of cloud-based app
adoption and the growth

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.160
of cloud workers, the
importance of the browser

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.460
has elevated across
the enterprises.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:37.610
The browser is no
longer a passive tool

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.350
used to browse data.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.440
It's now the crux of
where business users go

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.870
to access the critical
applications they

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.334
need to be productive.

00:02:47.334 --> 00:02:49.000
I know when I go to
work in the morning,

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.030
the browser is the
first thing that I open.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.725
And for people like
me, the browser

00:02:53.725 --> 00:02:55.960
has actually become my desktop.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.500
It's the place I go to get my
work done and to be productive.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.540
Because people are
spending so much more

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.630
time in their browser, it's
more important than ever

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:10.304
before for IT to really have
a strategy around the benefits

00:03:10.304 --> 00:03:11.220
of the modern browser.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.950
When we talk to
our customers who

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.070
are adopting Chrome Browser
in their enterprise,

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:23.850
the benefits that they find
are really in three key areas.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.040
Let's talk about management.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.540
Chrome Browser makes it simple
for IT to manage through

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:31.820
their existing management tools.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:34.610
For example, you can
manage hundreds of policies

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.140
through group policy.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.090
We make these
templates available.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:41.810
And I'll show you exactly where
you can find those later on.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.450
For Mac users, we
offer documentation

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.970
on how you can manage
through PLISTs.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:51.230
And we also offer the option of
managing your browser instances

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:54.410
right from the cloud in
the Google Admin console.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:56.300
We'll be opening
up this capability

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.420
to IT admins that aren't
Google Admins today

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:00.830
in the coming months.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:04.220
At that point, we'll have
a single destination for IT

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.530
to manage Chrome
Browser instances

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:10.800
across all of the different
platforms in their environment.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.080
Security is also critical.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.320
Chrome Browser is constantly
increasing built-in security

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.990
capabilities for its
broader user base.

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:22.570
And those benefits extend
to the enterprise as well.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:26.730
For example, the Safe Browsing
service offered by Google

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.430
helps users be
prevented from visiting

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.970
infected or potentially
malicious websites.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:37.200
And our Sandboxing and Site
Isolation capabilities ensures

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.030
that threats cannot spread
from within or between tabs,

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.310
making sure that these
threats cannot spread across

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.050
your network.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:50.610
With IT's ability to enforce
and add additional policies

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:53.850
and controls, enterprises
find that they can get Chrome

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:56.610
to a secure state that means
even the most stringent

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.870
security requirements.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:01.240
And of course, there's
your happy users.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.000
Chrome Browser offers
a familiar experience

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.860
that your users love.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.080
So they can pick up
right where they left off

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.780
and be productive across
all of the different devices

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.320
that they use in
their working day.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.660
At the end of the
day, whether we're

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:20.630
talking about web browsers
or any technology decision,

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:22.960
we know that IT is
constantly balancing

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:27.040
two very important requirements,
security and the user

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:28.820
experience.

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:32.810
While IT teams and security
teams might see this lockdown

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:37.250
state as an ideal state
from a security perspective,

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:40.470
user experience simply
can't fall to the wayside.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.560
And we know that IT folks agree.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.390
In a recent Forrester survey,
73% of IT professionals

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:51.200
said that employee experience
is a top priority when

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.100
they make technology
decisions in their enterprise.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:58.550
Chrome Browser really helps
IT teams strike this balance

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:01.590
between users and security.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.250
And auto-updates
are no exception.

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:07.340
You're here today to learn more
about the Chrome Browser update

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:08.090
model.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:11.330
And it's a great example
of how IT teams can promote

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:14.600
both the latest security
capabilities while offering

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.150
a great user experience.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:20.120
Automatic updates are
standard for Chrome Browser.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.100
And let's look at
exactly why that's

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:26.300
our primary recommendation
for enterprises.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.040
Chrome's commitment
to delivering

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.520
continuous enhancements
provides a fast and easy way

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:34.050
to keep your
employees productive.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:37.410
All of that is alongside timely
vulnerability mitigations

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:39.650
and new security capabilities.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:42.260
So with auto-updates
running, your enterprises

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:45.890
get fast and instant access
to the latest browser

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:49.160
with the most advanced
protections available to them.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:52.130
And with auto-updates, you
eliminate the need for IT

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:54.200
to handle manual
patching efforts,

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.870
freeing up IT's
time and resources.

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:02.940
And there's great
benefits for users, too.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:06.510
For example, they can get
an optimized experience

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:09.420
on the modern web apps that
they use by using the latest

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:12.160
version of the browser.

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:14.040
And if all your
users are running

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.200
on the same version
of the browser,

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.380
it makes it easy for you to
handle any potential support

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:22.440
issues or challenges
that might arise.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.290
And for the users, the updates
themselves are seamless.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.600
It just takes a simple
refresh, and they're

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.000
guaranteed to be on the
latest version that's

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.210
available to them.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:35.470
And for our customers who've
opted into Chrome enterprise

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:39.130
support, they get
24/7 access to Chrome

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:41.710
experts to help them with
their deployment and browser

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:43.081
installations.

00:07:43.081 --> 00:07:44.830
And they'll have the
peace of mind knowing

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.110
that their users are
on the latest supported

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:50.250
version of the browser.

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:52.970
You might be wondering how
Chrome Browser's update

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:56.660
model compares to other
modern or legacy browsers.

00:07:56.660 --> 00:08:01.050
Chrome sets the pace in browser
updates for the industry.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:04.090
But we'll let the metrics
speak for themselves.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:07.410
We always encourage fast
and frequent auto-updates

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.180
as best practice.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.850
But we know some enterprises
have unique testing

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:13.890
or internal
requirements that might

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:17.430
make it challenging to stay
on automatic update models.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:19.410
So we do have some
options available

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:22.000
for you to manage this process.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.520
Let's take a look at
these more closely.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:27.515
Even if your organization
has the desire

00:08:27.515 --> 00:08:29.810
to benefit from
auto-updates, you

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:31.400
could have some
internal challenges

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:35.159
that make this difficult.
Some of those common things

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:39.390
include concerns around
application compatibility,

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:43.559
unique testing requirements,
change management processes

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:48.790
that you're required to follow,
internal security reviews,

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:51.340
and even regulatory
requirements.

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:54.420
If, for these reasons
or others, auto-updates

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.850
aren't exactly for
your organization,

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:59.862
you can turn off the
auto-update functionality.

00:08:59.862 --> 00:09:02.070
We just want to make sure
that you're aware that it's

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:04.000
a bit of a trade-off.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:07.080
You may not be running with
the latest security releases.

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:10.660
And when it comes to UI
changes or other enhancements,

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:12.880
your users may not be getting
the latest and greatest

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:16.040
experience that
we have to offer.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.660
We'll also cover some options
you have for looking ahead

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:20.240
to the Beta channel,
because there

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.520
are some great ways that you can
take advantage of early access

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.000
to that Beta channel to get
ahead on some of the testing

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.660
or internal requirements
that might make auto-updates

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:35.450
a little bit more viable
for your organization.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:37.600
Let's talk about the
Chrome release cycles

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:40.680
so you know exactly
what to expect.

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:44.460
The Chrome release cycle
runs roughly every six weeks,

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:49.500
moving from Beta into Stable in
time for the next Beta release.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.860
The ideal time for
you to test your apps

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:55.140
is during those first two
weeks of a Beta release.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.180
If you find any
issues with Chrome,

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:59.070
you should immediately
report them to Google

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.352
within this two-week window.

00:10:01.352 --> 00:10:02.810
That will help us
ensure that we're

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.330
delivering a stable
version that's

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:07.235
going to be great for
you and your users.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:12.900
And Chrome Browser has
multiple release channels.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.190
You have access to
all of these, and you

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:18.220
can access them as early
as your organization wants.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:21.700
By leveraging the various
release channels and testing,

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.950
you can be in an
auto-update world

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:27.010
while still having the
opportunity to adequately test

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:28.900
in your enterprise.

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.300
All of these channels,
ranging from Chrome Canary

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:34.300
across to Chrome Stable,
are available to you.

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:37.090
At a minimum, we recommend
that your organization

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:40.032
leverages the Beta version
for testing internally.

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:46.650
Each stable release marks
a major version release.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:50.550
And each Chrome platform has a
different stable rollout plan.

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:52.080
It's important to
note that this can

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:54.210
vary based on circumstances.

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:56.520
But this is a good general
guide that can help you

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.140
with your planning.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:01.350
Today we're mostly going
to focus on desktops.

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:06.610
The desktop consists of three
main platforms, Windows, Macs,

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:08.450
and Linux.

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:12.800
Linux is ramped up
100% immediately.

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:14.800
Mac and Windows,
on the other hand

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:22.010
follow a staged rollout plan,
starting at 5%, moving to 15%,

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:26.960
going up to 50%, and
then reaching 100%.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:28.850
You may notice this
rollout process

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:32.840
within your organization if
you're using auto-updates.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:36.140
This staging process allows
for any potential issues

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:37.940
to be detected early.

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:40.100
In a sense, we're
offering your organization

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.380
a built-in safeguard
for auto-updates.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:48.060
We've talked about
testing quite a bit here.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:50.880
But how should you go
about internal testing?

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.430
We have a few tips for you.

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:56.220
First off, you should be
leveraging release notes that

00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:57.960
are available on the
Help Center to help

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:00.270
you understand what's new.

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:03.540
They're released within a few
weeks of the Beta release,

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:06.300
and they highlight
any new functionality

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:09.210
that could be of interest
to your enterprise.

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:12.000
In some cases, we'll even
highlight future changes,

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.690
coming and upcoming releases
so that you can plan ahead.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:19.260
We also suggest that you
enable a group of users

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:22.170
within your organization
to become testers.

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:24.720
You might be surprised
at how some users are

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.480
interested in wanting to be
a part of these early testing

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:29.750
groups.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:33.130
We also recommend that you
consider some automated testing

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:37.760
around features and applications
that are leveraging Chrome.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:41.510
Overall, it's a good idea to
invest your internal testing

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:44.300
resources in Chrome as well.

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:46.100
We know a lot of
organizations have

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:50.570
dedicated teams that focus
on testing on premise apps.

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:53.870
The browser itself
sometimes gets forgotten.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:57.030
And with each update,
Chrome Browser is changing.

00:12:57.030 --> 00:13:00.380
So as your users rely
more on the browser,

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:02.630
it makes sense to have
some resources that

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:07.040
have ownership over the testing
of their apps on Chrome Browser

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:09.260
and the browser itself.

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:11.120
No matter what,
the best thing is

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:15.510
to find a process or cycle that
works for your organization.

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:17.450
You might find that
auto-updates are actually

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:19.940
easier for your team to
manage than you thought.

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:26.720
If we look at a great way
we see some organizations

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:30.230
segment their users and
maximize their use of updates,

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:32.560
it looks something like this.

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:35.940
There might be a small
group of select Beta users.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:39.160
They're serving as an
internal source of QA,

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:41.950
really testing out the
browser latest release

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:46.510
on the applications and things
that they do on a daily basis.

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:50.020
Then the majority of your users
will be on the Stable version,

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.570
running auto-updates,
always making sure

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:56.170
that they're on the latest and
greatest version of Chrome.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:59.360
Then you might have
a much smaller group

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:01.730
of exception users
that happen to be

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:04.280
pinned on a version of Chrome.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:07.760
Again, we really encourage
this being the exception.

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:09.740
We'll talk a little
bit more about that

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:12.670
among all of your options.

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:15.310
Across the board, you
can approach updates

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:18.500
with Chrome in four
different ways.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:21.320
If you haven't caught on
yet, our recommendation

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:24.420
is to keep auto-updates
on and enabled.

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:27.560
It's the default. And we
make additional policies

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:29.990
available to help you make
sure these updates actually

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:31.410
take place.

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:33.770
For example, you
can use a policy

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:37.260
that designates users to refresh
their browser within a given

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:38.360
time frame.

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:40.619
That makes sure they don't
miss any critical updates

00:14:40.619 --> 00:14:42.410
because they haven't
refreshed or restarted

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:44.900
their browser in a while.

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:49.640
Another option you have is to
disable auto-updates, but issue

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:52.280
periodic updates on your own.

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:55.220
Most organizations handle this
through a third-party software

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:57.950
distribution tool,
using manual GPO

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:01.550
updates, or other processes.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:04.320
You can also disable
updates entirely,

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:07.610
pinning your user to
a specific version.

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:10.610
As discussed before, this
is really only recommended

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:12.270
to be an exception.

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:14.810
And we want to make sure
that you're limiting this

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:17.360
so that folks are getting
the maximum security and user

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:20.100
experience benefits from Chrome.

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:23.400
In some cases, you might need
to take a mixed approach.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:26.220
You might need to have some
of these pinned users based

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.380
on a very specific
business need,

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:31.590
whether it's an application
requirement or some sort

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:33.960
of security and
compliance reason,

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:35.640
while your broader
group can still

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:39.235
be benefiting from the use
of auto-updates or scheduled

00:15:39.235 --> 00:15:39.735
updates.

00:15:43.189 --> 00:15:44.980
We're going to quickly
show you how you can

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:47.810
get started managing updates.

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:50.860
I'm going to visit
chrome.com/enterprise,

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:54.340
which is the download page that
includes all of the downloads,

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:57.430
files, and templates and more
that you need to manage Chrome

00:15:57.430 --> 00:15:59.186
Browser in your enterprise.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:07.670
Let's focus on Windows.

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:09.770
You'll see that we
have enterprise bundles

00:16:09.770 --> 00:16:11.930
and standalone MSI
files, which you

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:16.040
would need to retrieve if you
decide to disable auto-updates.

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:18.890
We make templates available
for Google updates.

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:21.290
This is for updates
across Google Services,

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:23.020
including Chrome.

00:16:23.020 --> 00:16:25.190
On the next tab, you
can see documentation

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:27.710
that we make available for
managing those updates.

00:16:27.710 --> 00:16:30.200
It will guide you through
the downloading process

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:32.960
and the process for installing
templates into the Windows

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:35.580
Policy Definitions folder.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:38.076
On the next tab, you'll
see some GitHub files

00:16:38.076 --> 00:16:39.950
that one of our Chrome
deployment specialists

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:41.720
has made available.

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:45.230
This script will kick off the
download of the GPO templates,

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:48.260
unzip, and install them into
the Windows Policy Definitions

00:16:48.260 --> 00:16:49.870
folder.

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:52.330
This is an alternative to
the manual steps outlined

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:53.410
in the previous steps.

00:16:55.930 --> 00:16:59.380
From here, you'll be able
to view policy options

00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:02.500
around disabling
auto-updates, dive into Policy

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:06.280
Settings to set
auto-updates, manage timing

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:09.940
of your auto-updates, and
you can enforce policies

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:12.460
like the one that prompts
your users to restart

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:21.859
to trigger a browser update

00:17:21.859 --> 00:17:23.869
Let's suppose you've
decided that you

00:17:23.869 --> 00:17:25.730
want to use software
distribution

00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:28.069
for your initial
deployment of Chrome.

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:30.640
This is a common scenario.

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:33.480
The steps would look a
little something like this.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:35.950
You would go through an
initial discovery process

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:39.070
to understand what's
currently on your desktops.

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:42.010
Perhaps there are users
of versions of Chrome

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:45.260
that were
self-installed by users.

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:48.260
Once you've done this discovery,
you'll want to build out

00:17:48.260 --> 00:17:52.460
your enterprise Chrome package
using the MSI that we saw when

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:56.060
we visited
chrome.com/enterprise.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:59.540
The next thing you want to do is
identify a group of pilot users

00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:01.520
to install this on.

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:05.030
Based on the feedback you
receive and any of the change

00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:07.250
management or release
management processes

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:11.630
you need to go through, you
can move through that step.

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:14.090
Then you'll move on
to installing Chrome

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:17.390
into your production
environment.

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:21.050
Once you've done that rollout,
you can focus on updates

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:22.850
and really put yourself
in maintenance mode

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:25.680
around the browser.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:29.010
As you can see, if you've
completed that cycle

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:31.710
and decided to leave
updates on, you'll

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:36.255
really only need to focus on
the feedback and the updates.

00:18:36.255 --> 00:18:38.130
And you'll have the
assurance that, every six

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:40.505
weeks, your enterprise is
getting the latest and greatest

00:18:40.505 --> 00:18:41.970
updates.

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:43.870
If you decide to
disable auto-updates,

00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:47.490
though, you can plan to
issue periodic updates.

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:49.800
But you'll look at repeating
many of these same steps

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:52.570
again for each time you update.

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:55.750
As you can see, you can get
significant time savings

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.460
by staying on the
auto-update model.

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:03.010
Another topic that
comes up around updates

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:05.470
is around the ADM,
ADMX templates

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:07.720
that we make available.

00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:11.860
With each release, new GPO
templates become available.

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:14.200
That's often because we're
introducing new policies

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:16.940
around management and control.

00:19:16.940 --> 00:19:19.210
So if you see that there's
new policies that you

00:19:19.210 --> 00:19:21.640
want to take advantage
of in a given release,

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:24.640
you'll need to visit that
same Chrome Enterprise site

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:26.947
and install these
files separately.

00:19:26.947 --> 00:19:28.780
At the end of this
session, I'll provide you

00:19:28.780 --> 00:19:32.260
a link to where you can see
an updated policy list, which

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:34.390
should serve as a
great guide for you

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:36.220
to review when you
want to determine

00:19:36.220 --> 00:19:38.950
when you're ready to go and
install new template updates.

00:19:42.630 --> 00:19:46.290
Another question we get is
around Legacy Browser Support.

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.420
Legacy Browser Support
is an extension

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:52.200
available to enterprises right
within the enterprise bundle.

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:56.520
It's a great tool that allows
you to predetermine which

00:19:56.520 --> 00:20:00.560
set of legacy apps, legacy
URLS, will open dynamically

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:01.960
in the legacy browser--

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:04.750
for example, Internet Explorer.

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:06.780
Enterprises love
Legacy Browser Support

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:10.140
because it allows them to keep
their user's primary browsing

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:13.350
experience within Chrome
Browser, where they can benefit

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:15.120
from all the
security capabilities

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:17.610
and access their modern apps.

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:20.220
Then when a user goes
to use a legacy app,

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:22.170
they'll automatically
get redirected

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:24.300
into Internet Explorer.

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:26.670
And once they're ready to
browse or use a different app,

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:29.130
they'll be redirected
dynamically

00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:30.810
right back into Chrome.

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:32.550
This is great for
these scenarios

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:34.680
where an enterprise
is living partway

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:38.870
between a modern and
a legacy app world.

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:41.420
This extension is
updated periodically.

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:43.517
But it's not included
in the auto-updates.

00:20:43.517 --> 00:20:45.350
So you'll just want to
make sure that you're

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:47.780
planning on reviewing
those updates

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:49.430
and distributing
them accordingly

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:53.120
as it makes sense for
your organization.

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:55.010
Legacy Browser
Support is an example

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:57.620
of one popular extension.

00:20:57.620 --> 00:20:59.440
And it's a Google extension.

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:01.730
But we know, in your
enterprise, you probably

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:04.370
have quite a few
third-party extensions

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:07.220
that your users love to use
in their environment to help

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:09.790
make them more productive.

00:21:09.790 --> 00:21:11.890
Managing updates for
third-party extensions

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:14.200
comes up sometimes too.

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:17.170
Extension management is built
into the Chrome enterprise

00:21:17.170 --> 00:21:18.190
bundle.

00:21:18.190 --> 00:21:20.110
There's a variety of
different policies

00:21:20.110 --> 00:21:24.200
that really lets you control
access to extensions.

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:27.130
You can even get very granular
around permission controls

00:21:27.130 --> 00:21:29.680
that are allowed around
these extensions.

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:33.070
But when it comes to updating
them, you have a few choices.

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:35.920
The web store can
allow for auto-updates.

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:39.580
Or you can decide to pin
extensions in your environment.

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:41.590
Your organization
should really decide

00:21:41.590 --> 00:21:45.640
what approach makes sense
around managing and updating

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:47.511
the extensions in
their environment.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:52.930
We've covered a
lot of information.

00:21:52.930 --> 00:21:55.115
But what can your
teams be doing today?

00:21:55.115 --> 00:21:58.380
And what early next
steps can you take?

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:01.380
Well, for starters, it's
a great conversation

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:04.620
to have around what your
bandwidth and tolerance is

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:07.760
for new updates within
your organization.

00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:10.040
There's really a
trade-off that happens.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:13.160
Do you want the security
enhancements guaranteed

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:15.200
with the latest
version of the browser,

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:20.429
or would you rather control
timing of updates on your own?

00:22:20.429 --> 00:22:22.220
Your team should spend
a little bit of time

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:23.990
getting familiar with
some of the release

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:26.000
cycles I mentioned today.

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:29.180
Really determine how you can
possibly maximize testing

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:32.300
within your organization
based on the different release

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:34.080
cycles.

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:35.940
You can also see if
there is an opportunity

00:22:35.940 --> 00:22:38.230
to segment your users.

00:22:38.230 --> 00:22:40.200
In a lot of
organizations, there's

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:44.310
a group of users that would
do great on auto-updates.

00:22:44.310 --> 00:22:48.060
That way, if you're deciding
to manage or pin updates,

00:22:48.060 --> 00:22:50.600
it's really for an
isolated group of users.

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:52.350
That will take the
burden off of your team

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:55.944
when it comes to managing
and securing the browser.

00:22:55.944 --> 00:22:57.360
And of course,
you'll want to make

00:22:57.360 --> 00:22:59.850
sure that you're reviewing
the enterprise release notes

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:03.020
that we make available
to you often and early.

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:04.530
It will give you
great visibility

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:07.440
into changes coming ahead
that your organization can

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:08.788
plan accordingly.

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:16.180
I've talked about a lot
of information today.

