00:25:37.120 --> 00:25:39.090
Let's take a look at
some common questions

00:25:39.090 --> 00:25:43.640
that we get around managing
Google Chrome updates.

00:25:43.640 --> 00:25:46.340
One question we get is
whether release notes

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:49.240
cover all updates.

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:50.710
That's a good question.

00:25:50.710 --> 00:25:53.710
What our enterprise release
notes really aim to do

00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:57.060
is look at some of the
changes to Chrome Browser,

00:25:57.060 --> 00:25:59.950
the policies, and
relevant extensions

00:25:59.950 --> 00:26:02.650
that have the potential
to impact your enterprise.

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:06.220
We try and highlight and
focus on those major changes.

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:08.830
So while you won't get
an exhaustive list of all

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:12.010
the changes to Chrome Browser,
this is a great resource

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:13.960
and a great starting
point to help you

00:26:13.960 --> 00:26:17.380
as you plan out your testing
and rollouts of new versions

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:20.040
of Chrome Browser.

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:24.000
Another question we got is
around the stage rollouts.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:27.570
We've had some enterprises ask
if they can opt specific users

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:30.030
into getting immediate
rollouts instead of waiting

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:32.200
for that stage rollout.

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:35.320
Unfortunately, the stage
rollout works in a way

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:40.150
so that we're able to account
for any early potential issues

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:42.040
and challenges and address them.

00:26:42.040 --> 00:26:46.250
So there's no way to designate
exactly who those users are.

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:49.090
However, if you do
have a group of users

00:26:49.090 --> 00:26:51.490
that you would like
on the latest version,

00:26:51.490 --> 00:26:54.280
they might make for a great
option as your Beta testing

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:54.780
group.

00:26:57.450 --> 00:26:59.700
Another question that
sometimes comes up

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:03.240
is around our enterprise
support offering

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:05.250
and whether some Google
customers already

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:10.140
have access to Chrome enterprise
support for the browser.

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:12.240
The support offering
that I've talked about

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:16.710
is a standalone offering, and
it gives folks 24/7 access

00:27:16.710 --> 00:27:19.410
to reach out to Chrome
Browser specialists

00:27:19.410 --> 00:27:23.310
that can help answer questions
about their deployments.

00:27:23.310 --> 00:27:25.680
If you do happen
to have G Suite,

00:27:25.680 --> 00:27:28.770
you already have
access to this service.

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:30.630
But if you're just
using Chrome Browser

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:33.640
and don't have other contracts
with Google in place,

00:27:33.640 --> 00:27:36.750
this is a great option for
how IT can work directly

00:27:36.750 --> 00:27:38.910
with Google to make
sure Chrome Browser is

00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:42.540
successful in their environment.

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:45.320
We also sometimes get questions
around managing updates

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:46.820
for Mac users.

00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:49.010
You probably noticed that
most of today's session

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:51.620
really focused on
Windows environments,

00:27:51.620 --> 00:27:54.020
because we know that it is
predominantly where most

